**PDF 1: Journal of Controlled Release 204 (2015) 51-59**

This study investigates dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticles (Nano-11), derived from chemically modified phytoglycogen, as vaccine adjuvants. Nano-11 particles are spherical (75-80 nm), positively charged, and efficiently adsorb negatively charged protein antigens. In vitro, they exhibit low cytotoxicity, enhance antigen uptake by dendritic cells (DCs) via phagocytosis, and activate DCs by increasing costimulatory molecule expression and secreting IL-1β and IL-12p40. In vivo, Nano-11 formulations significantly boost antigen-specific IgG responses to different proteins while inducing a mild, transient local inflammatory response that resolves quickly.

**PDF 2: npj Vaccines (2017) 2:4**

This paper details the in vivo kinetics and immune cell targeting of Nano-11, a cationic dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticle, as a vaccine adjuvant. Nano-11 demonstrated comparable adjuvanticity to aluminum hydroxide, especially at lower doses, inducing robust antibody titers. It remained localized at the injection site, clearing gradually over three weeks without systemic distribution. Nano-11 induced a transient local inflammatory response dominated by macrophages/monocytes. Importantly, it was efficiently taken up by recruited macrophages at the injection site and transported by migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs) to draining lymph nodes, significantly enhancing antigen delivery to these immunopotentiating cells.

